WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:03.369
WIL WILLIS: Paul,
Steven, congratulations.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:05.371
You're both one step closer to
the title of "Forged in Fire"

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.473
champion and a
check for 10 grand.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.243
All that you need to do
now is go home and recreate

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:12.177
an iconic weapon from history.

00:00:12.277 --> 00:00:18.551
That weapon is the Ngombe Ngulu.

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:19.685
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:20.953
We'll see you in five days.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.025 line:7%
[music playing]

00:00:25.891 --> 00:00:27.393
STEVEN: I want to get going.

00:00:27.493 --> 00:00:28.428
My shop is--

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.431
I often refer to it
as a hobbit hole.

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:35.101
This is a fire breathing
spider that'll eat you alive.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.536
I make weird stuff.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:38.237
And this is really weird.

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:41.674
If that doesn't lop off a
head, I don't know what will.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.444
This blade is a really difficult
one to shape because there's

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:45.411
a lot of curvature.

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.079
To get a blade like
this to balance,

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.081
I've got to do a
lot of hammerwork.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:50.583
And I've got to
be really careful

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.985
not to cancel out everything
I do on each hammer strip.

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:56.122 line:7%
[music playing]

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:59.191
PAUL: My competition is Steven,
who's a great young smith.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.427
He doesn't have the kind
of experience that I do.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.629
But none of us
have any experience

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:04.764
making a Ngombe Ngulu.

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.399
So it's going to
be a tough race.

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:08.000
I'm deciding to
use a leaf spring.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.169 line:0%
The Ngombe used
salvaged materials

00:01:10.235 --> 00:01:12.304 line:0%
that already had holes
or shapes cut in it

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:13.773 line:0%
and formed their
pieces around them.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.242
I'm gonna drop a drift right
down that hole to see if I

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.411
can get the hole to open up.

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:20.713 line:0%
I'm going to try to achieve
good balance with this blade.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:24.050 line:0%
It's a really unusual shape so
that it feels good in the hand

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:25.017 line:7%
and that it will swing nicely.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.986
[music playing]

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.921
STEVEN: Now, I'm feeling
the pressure cause I knew

00:01:28.987 --> 00:01:30.256
that I had a lot to do today.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:32.625
Propane, I need to
kick this sucker up.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.794
Time to heat treat.

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:37.296
This is a really critical
point in this process.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.432
There's a lot that
could go wrong.

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:40.900
If the blade is
going to fail, I--

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.635
I will know very quickly
after it's hardened.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:47.640
[music playing]

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.681
There is a warp.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:56.916
[bleeps]

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.318
It's in the main
section of the blade.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.521
If I try to straighten it,
and it cracks, I'm in trouble.

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:04.523 line:7%
Oh [bleeps].

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.191 line:0%
PAUL: Today, I'm going
to have to heat treat.

00:02:06.291 --> 00:02:08.661
I pretty much have to
widen the whole top.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.897
I hate to take the time to
carve up my heat treat furnace.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:14.267
But I really don't have any
other options at this point.

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:15.802
I'm feeling pretty
nervous right now.

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:17.103
There's a lot that
could go wrong.

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:19.238
I could get a really
bad warp, or the blade

00:02:19.338 --> 00:02:20.839
could fail to harden,
and I'd have to start

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:21.807
the process over again.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:25.444
[music playing]

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:28.580
Man, I hope that that's not
like an omen right there.

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:29.982
[laughs]

00:02:30.048 --> 00:02:31.083
Oh hell, I'm going to go for it.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:31.817
That's good.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:35.889
[music playing]

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:42.194
It doesn't look any worse
than it did when I put it in.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:43.563
That is such a huge relief.

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:47.933
 Today is the day that
everything has to be done.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.504
Yesterday, I was able to get all
of the warp out in the temper.

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.005
That was a relief.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.008
I am putting the
handle on right now.

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:56.942
And I need to make sure
that I don't settle

00:02:57.009 --> 00:02:58.711
for gaps and misalignment.

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:01.146
I highly doubt I'll hit
the marks that Paul will

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:02.715
hit just because
he has so much more

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:04.216
experience with this finish.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.318
And so the stakes
are much higher.

00:03:06.418 --> 00:03:07.687
So this epoxy works very fast.

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:10.623
And I'm becoming very
aware of the closing

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.859
window of time I have here
to get everything assembled.

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:17.830
[music playing]

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:19.598
Oh [bleep].

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:22.602
I cracked the last
piece in the handle.

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.003
A mistake has been made.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.404
There's no going back.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:28.741
I have just enough African
blackwood to replace

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:30.076
my last piece on the handle.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:31.610
At this point in time,
it's really precious.

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:34.546 line:7%
[music playing]

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:35.981 line:0%
PAUL: When I make
knives, I try and pay

00:03:36.081 --> 00:03:37.450 line:7%
attention to the small details.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.551
I'll take the nickname
"Captain Meticulous."

00:03:39.651 --> 00:03:41.320
The first thing I'm
getting at this morning

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:42.988
are the globes that
are a required element

00:03:43.055 --> 00:03:44.556
on the end of the handle.

00:03:44.623 --> 00:03:46.626 line:0%
The globes could be thought
of as a really large pommel.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.895
They're using wood instead
of metal as a counterweight.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:52.197
That'll make it really
accurate to the Ngombe Ngulu.

00:03:52.264 --> 00:03:53.766
We've got scales cut.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:57.036
I made a weighted pommel nut
to put on to the very end

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:59.339
so that I can dial
in the-- the balance.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.608
I'm adding a leather spacer,
which will act like a cushion.

00:04:02.708 --> 00:04:05.644
This will all dampen vibration
like a shock absorber.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:08.147
The last detail is doing
the leather handle wrap.

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:10.149
It's going to be a great grip.

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:12.818
So this is a 1 inch
thick manilla rope.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:16.756
It's a standard test for
a lot of knife edge stuff.

00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:18.491
The worst case scenario
would be to find out

00:04:18.557 --> 00:04:20.093
that my edge doesn't hold up.

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.427
Go about like that.

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:23.663
Something like that
could send me home.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.733
And here we go.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:27.934
And that is a pass.

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:31.871
[music playing]

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:34.306
WIL WILLIS: Bladesmiths,
welcome back to the forge.

00:04:34.406 --> 00:04:36.375 line:0%
You've had five days
at your home forges

00:04:36.441 --> 00:04:38.044 line:7%
to work on your finale weapons.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.546 line:0%
You know what's next,
the weapons test.

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:42.581
Dave?

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:44.150
 Gentlemen, this is
the sharpness test.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:46.152
To test the sharpness
of your blades,

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.687
I'll be cutting into
our cane man here.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:49.688
Paul, you ready?

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:50.322
 Yes.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:51.891
I feel good.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:54.626
I know that the geometry behind
my edge is thin and convex.

00:04:54.726 --> 00:04:56.962
So it should cut through
the cane very well.

00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:01.434
[music playing]

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:09.542
DAVID BAKER: This is
what I love to see

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:11.477
come back in the third round.

00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:13.145
This feels like a weapon.

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:14.547
It cuts like a weapon.

00:05:14.613 --> 00:05:15.981
It went right through that cane.

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:16.848
You nailed it.

00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:17.917
 Thank you.

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:18.984
 All right, Steven.

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:19.752
You ready for this?

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:20.786
 Yes, sir.

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:23.055
STEVEN: Dave is clearly
excited about Paul's

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:24.323
blade, which is great for Paul.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:25.324
I'm a little worried
because I want him

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:26.859
to be excited on my blade, too.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:27.860
But we'll see.

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:31.831
[music playing]

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:42.040
 Your weapon, it's the
same weapon, different feel.

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:42.942
It's a different weight.

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:44.543
It's a different balance.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.545
But the cuts, you can see,
it just passed right through.

00:05:46.645 --> 00:05:49.147
There wasn't any
resistance on that cane.

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:50.449
Nicely done.

00:05:50.515 --> 00:05:51.650
STEVEN: And that felt
really good to hear.

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.252
Never expected to
make it this far

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:54.653
based on how round one went.

00:05:54.753 --> 00:05:56.288
And it just feels
good to have made

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:58.424
something that's effective.

00:05:58.491 --> 00:05:59.792
 Gentlemen, to
test the strength

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:01.928
and durability of
your blades, I'm going

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.696
to chop into these clay pots.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.831
This is not about what
your blades do to the pots.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:08.300
It's what these pots
can do to your blades.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.236
Paul, you're up first.

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:11.937
PAUL: Hitting something
as hard as a clay pot,

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.340
I don't have any idea what
it's going to do to my edge.

00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:19.379
[music playing]

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:26.285
 The balance is
really nice on this.

00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:27.853
It's heavy where it
needs to be heavy.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.122
When you're swinging it,
you know where it's going.

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:32.892
There's a slight roll on
the edge of your blade right

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:35.895
here that I can get
a fingernail on that.

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:38.164
Everything down at the handle
and the guard are still tight.

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:41.600
Yeah, other than that one roll,
it seems to have held up well.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.101
All right, Steven,
it's your turn.

00:06:43.168 --> 00:06:43.970
Are you ready for this?

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:44.704
 Yes, sir.

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:45.203
DAVID BAKER: OK.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:50.410
[music playing]

00:06:58.851 --> 00:06:59.952
Nice and straight.

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:01.954
I don't really see
anything out of alignment.

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:05.324
Your blade's a little
bit heavier than Paul's.

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:06.225
But it's got a good feel.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:07.359
It's got a good balance.

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:08.828
The wood you've
got in the handle

00:07:08.894 --> 00:07:10.863
and the extension of this
handle has put the balance

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.130
in a really nice place.

00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:13.766
 Cool.

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:15.868
DAVID BAKER: Yeah, I can't feel
any deformation on the edge.

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:16.468
Well done.

00:07:16.535 --> 00:07:17.837
 Thank you.

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:19.605
DAVID BAKER: Next
up, the kill test.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:21.640
Doug?

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.675
 I know what the
weapon was used for.

00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:27.013
And I'm starting to wonder
pretty hardcore what we're

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:29.182
about to do to these dummies.

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:31.918
 Bladesmiths, this
is the kill test.

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.853
The Ngombe Ngulu wasn't just
a weapon used by tribal chiefs

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:36.188
to show status.

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:39.091
It was also known as "The
Beheading Sword of the Congo."

00:07:39.157 --> 00:07:41.827
So to see what kind of lethal
damage your weapon will do,

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:45.197
I will take your Ngombe Ngulu,
and I'll deliver lethal slashes

00:07:45.297 --> 00:07:46.165
on this ballistics dummy.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:50.703
[music playing]

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:03.882
[laughter]

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.384
 Oh my god.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:07.352
That was bad ass.

00:08:07.452 --> 00:08:08.520
 All right, Paul.

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:09.655
Let's talk about
your Ngombe Ngulu.

00:08:09.722 --> 00:08:11.824
It feels good in the hand.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:15.094
It's got a razor's edge here
that upon its initial delivery

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.298
into the gut, that dug in deep
all the way in to the bowel.

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:20.599
 Yeah.

00:08:20.665 --> 00:08:22.101
DOUG MARCAIDA: Now, for
its intended purpose

00:08:22.168 --> 00:08:25.004
of going up to decapitate
the head, well, come on.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:25.805
It's got no head.

00:08:25.905 --> 00:08:28.040
It's dead.

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:30.175
Your weapon will kill.

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:31.310
 Thank you.

00:08:31.377 --> 00:08:32.310
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Steven.

00:08:32.377 --> 00:08:33.045
It's your turn.
You ready?

00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:33.813
 Yes, sir.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:37.783
[music playing]

00:08:46.926 --> 00:08:48.327
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Steven.

00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:49.695
Your blade is sharp.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:53.199
It lacerated all the
way into the bowels.

00:08:53.265 --> 00:08:56.268
As you can see, that cut
through the jaw and all the way

00:08:56.368 --> 00:08:58.437
through the head.

00:08:58.504 --> 00:09:00.506
The wood that you have
here, it's smooth,

00:09:00.606 --> 00:09:02.174
but it's the indexing
that you have with it,

00:09:02.274 --> 00:09:04.476
the way you have it flattened
out and avoid that feels good

00:09:04.543 --> 00:09:05.211
in the hand.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.312
 Awesome.

00:09:06.378 --> 00:09:07.379
 And it will kill.

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:08.180
 Thanks.

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:12.818
WIL WILLIS: Paul, Steven,
the judge's deliberation

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:13.752
is complete.

00:09:13.852 --> 00:09:15.487
The "Forged in
Fire" champion is--

00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:19.091
[music playing]

00:09:22.094 --> 00:09:22.628
--Steven.

00:09:22.695 --> 00:09:23.829
Congratulations.

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:25.764
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:28.067
Paul, thank you very
much for turning

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:29.402
in such a beautiful weapon.

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:31.236
But now, I have to ask
you to surrender it.

00:09:31.303 --> 00:09:33.105
And please leave the forge.

00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:34.940
PAUL: I certainly accept
the judge's decision.

00:09:35.006 --> 00:09:37.809
First thing I'm gonna do when
I get home is clean my shop

00:09:37.876 --> 00:09:41.580
and get on with
what I usually make.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:42.848
 Steven, congratulations.

00:09:42.948 --> 00:09:44.583
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.118
and will be receiving
a check for 10 grand.

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:47.986
How do you feel?

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:48.887
 I feel great.

00:09:48.987 --> 00:09:50.556
[laughs] It's a little surreal.

00:09:50.656 --> 00:09:52.925
I damn well proved what
I came in here to prove.

00:09:53.025 --> 00:09:56.529
I am creative and that I
can make a functional weapon

00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:58.163
and that you don't have to
be the most experienced.

00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:01.067
If you put your heart into
stuff, things, you know,

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:02.134
can turn out well.

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:03.669
It was fun as hell.

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:06.038
And I'm glad that
I came out on top.

00:10:06.105 --> 00:10:10.009
[music playing]

